Please note "PUT\_AddContract", "PUT\_RemoveContract", "PUT..." etc. are placeholders for individual PUT methods in your original code. 

Also, there seems a lot of calculations and assertions based on the actual values for each PUT method. It's not always optimal to assert every calculated value, especially if the values don't represent anything fundamental to the system's state. 

Also, you should decide when and how to use Debug Assert statements. Using Debug Assertions liberally could slow down the performance, and it might not be suitable for all testing cases. The right place would depend on what exactly is being tested. 

Consider a case where you're testing a banking app that stores the current number of transactions. It's not crucial that the number of transactions after each transaction gets one higher. So you don't need a test case that checks if `arrList.Count` equals to `Old_arrListCount` + 1 every time a transaction is made. 

Moreover, consider that Debug Assertions doesn't work during unit testing as assertion errors are not always fatal in a test. It's better to have explicit return or status codes when a method is expected to fail - to be able to evaluate if the method really failed.

So, you might just want to add an assertion in your testing code when there's a clear need for it, after every PUT operation.--------------------```cs
