```

The above line checks whether the index of a new added element is lesser than or equal to the size of the old ArrayList before adding a new item. This is a basic assertion check which can be replaced with actual error handling logic based on your needs.

Please note: Debug.Assert is a .NET framework assert, it isn't present by default, you have to do "using System.Diagnostics;" to use Debug.Assert.
If you want to use assertion instead of Debug.Assert, you could do:

```C#
if (!(New_Ret <= Old_arrListCount))
{
    throw new Exception("Assertion failed: New_Ret is greater than Old_arrListCount");
}
```
In this case, if assertion fails (meaning New_Ret is more than Old_arrListCount), your application will crash and there won't be any error message displayed.--------------------Sorry for any misunderstanding, but there's a syntax issue in the question. The code isn't asking for the assert statements corresponding to comments that start with "@@@". Instead, it's asking for the assert statements related to the comments "// @@@ last position of x in new array is lesser than or equal to old array size". To fix this, I'll try to create a simple csharp code that uses Debug.Assert to verify whether the last position of the added element is lesser than or equal to the old array size. Note: This is an example for one specific scenario, for multiple scenarios you might want to adjust this code.

Here's the code example:

```cs
using System;
using System.Diagnostics;

public class MainClass
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();
        arrList.Add(5);

        int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int Old_x = 5;

        arrList.Add(7); // Here we add 7 to the array

        int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

        Debug.Assert(arrList.LastIndexOf(Old_x) <= Old_arrListCount, "Last position of x in new array is greater than old array size");
        // Since last index (LastIndexOf) can change we verify with current old array size
    }
}
```
In this example `LastIndexOf(Old_x)` will return the last index of Old_x in the ArrayList. The `<=` comparison operator guarantees that the last position of the added element is lesser than or equal to the old array size. The second parameter of Debug.Assert is the message to display if the condition is false. This checks the condition and will pause the application if it fails.

Please note: The complete code provided here is only an example and it will throw an error at runtime because there is no full ArrayList class or LastIndexOf property in the .NET Framework.--------------------